    "content": "The Artemis II Orion capsule reaches a maximum speed of approximately 25,000 mph (40,000 km/h) during its reentry into Earth's atmosphere, making it one of the fastest human-rated flights in history. This high velocity ensures the capsule can survive the heat of reentry and return safely, falling from a record-setting distance, before parachutes slow it down to a 17 mph splashdown.Key Speed Milestones (Artemis 2):Reentry Speed: $\\sim$25,000 mph (approx. 40,000 km/h).Maximum Velocity: Reached just before entry interface.Trans-Lunar Injection: $\\sim$24,527 mph (39,472 km/h).Parachute Deployment: Slows capsule to $\\sim$300 mph, then 17 mph for landing.Mid-Space Cruise: $\\sim$17,000 mph.This video explains the extreme speeds the Artemis II mission will reach:06:48Sky News AustraliaYouTube• Apr 1, 2026The spacecraft experiences its highest speeds during two critical moments: exiting Earth's orbit for the moon and plunging back into the atmosphere. The heat shield protects the crew from temperatures up to 5,000 degrees Fahrenheit, which is half as hot as the visible surface of the sun.Watch this video for a detailed explanation of the speed of the Artemis II spacecraft:25sWIONYouTube• Apr 2, 2026 The Artemis II Orion capsule reaches a maximum speed of approximately 25,000 mph (40,000 km/h) during its reentry into Earth's atmosphere, making it one of the fastest human-rated flights in history. This high velocity ensures the capsule can survive the heat of reentry and return safely, falling from a record-setting distance, before parachutes slow it down to a 17 mph splashdown. Key Speed Milestones (Artemis 2): Reentry Speed: $\\sim$25,000 mph (approx. 40,000 km/h). Maximum Velocity: Reached just before entry interface. Trans-Lunar Injection: $\\sim$24,527 mph (39,472 km/h). Parachute Deployment: Slows capsule to $\\sim$300 mph, then 17 mph for landing. Mid-Space Cruise: $\\sim$17,000 mph. This video explains the extreme speeds the Artemis II mission will 